CRITTERS

Full Product, Game & Business Plan — Revised

Concept: An AI-powered virtual creature ecosystem combining Tamagotchi-style care, Pokémon-inspired battles, autonomous AI companions and Web3 ownership.

Core philosophy:

CRITTERS is not simply an NFT collection or another play-to-earn game. It is a living creature experience.

Every player hatches a unique creature that develops its own personality, remembers its experiences, reacts to its owner and progresses through its life.

Players care for their creatures, train them, send them on expeditions, compete in the arena and eventually witness their creature awaken into its ultimate form.

The original creature, its DNA, identity and history are never replaced.

The game is free to start. A player can care for a creature and fight for free. Revenue comes from a percentage of paid battle pots, and later from premium AI, optional content, and marketplace activity.

Existing $SOUL token holders receive additional gameplay and experience benefits based on their wallet holdings.

1. The Core Concept

CRITTERS combines four main ideas:

Tamagotchi: A creature that needs attention, food, rest and care.

Pokémon: Unique creatures with personality, attributes, progression and battles.

AI companions: Creatures that communicate, remember and develop individual behaviour.

Web3 ownership: A creature's identity and ownership are connected to a blockchain wallet.

The main differentiator is that the creature should feel alive rather than like a static NFT.

It should have its own personality, habits, moods, preferences and memories. It should react to what happens in its life and gradually develop a history with its owner.

The experience takes place on one screen. The player does not need another device, application or physical toy.

The three gameplay pillars

CRITTERS is built around three connected experiences:

Companion: Care, AI, personality, memory and awakening.

Arena: Battles, rankings, achievements and competitions.

Exploration: Expeditions, discoveries, resources and progression.

Everything in the game supports one of these pillars.

There are no separate robot games, machine-building systems or multiple disconnected battle modes.

2. The Creature System

2.1 Hatching

At launch, each wallet can hatch one creature.

The first species will be cats. Other species can be introduced later, each with their own appearance system and characteristics.

Every creature is generated from a deterministic seed. The seed is the wallet.

The same wallet always produces the same creature. Hatch, close the machine, and hatch again: the same cat, the same rarity, the same coat. A second attempt does not roll. There is no button that tries again until the result is legendary.

A different wallet is a different address, so it is a different creature. That is the only way to see another roll. It is still one creature for that wallet, still computed, still not a slot machine. Legendary is about one address in fifty, and a legendary coat does not win the table. Hunting addresses buys outfits, not champions.

Hatch itself does not call the AI and does not generate an image. The numbers and the coat are math. The model speaks later, when someone is actually keeping the creature. A wallet that hatches and leaves does not receive a conversation budget, stored portraits, or a stream of inference.

A bot can generate wallets at no cost. Those addresses are not creatures. A hatch exists only after that wallet signs the hatch transaction and it confirms. The bot pays the network fee and the account rent on every attempt. CRITTERS does not. Unsigned calls, repeated calls, and lists of fresh addresses are dropped before anything is stored.

What the bot still cannot collect:

A second roll on a wallet that already hatched.

A legendary that wins pots. The coat is rarer. The fight is the same.

A soul it can sell. The creature is soulbound.

An AI bill. Model calls start when a kept creature is spoken to, and a silent mint gets none.

The picture is computed from the seed when someone looks. It is not rendered into a file for every mint. A thousand abandoned hatches are a thousand small chain accounts the bot funded, and a thousand rows that do not call the model.

Each creature receives:

A unique name.

A species.

A rarity.

A coat and pattern.

Eye colour.

Accessories and mutations.

Six personality traits.

Initial gameplay attributes.

A unique identity and history.

The creature should feel individually generated rather than selected from a collection of predefined characters.

