3. The Living AI Companion

This is the heart of CRITTERS.

The creature should not simply display predetermined dialogue. It should feel like an individual character.

3.1 Personality-driven AI

The AI receives the creature's personality, current needs, memories, history and situation.

It uses these characteristics to determine how to respond.

Two creatures experiencing the same situation should be capable of responding differently.

For example:

One cat becomes excited when its owner returns.

Another ignores the owner initially.

One frequently asks for food.

Another becomes annoyed when interrupted.

One enjoys exploration.

Another prefers sleeping in its room.

The AI should never randomly change the creature's underlying identity or personality.

3.2 Memory

Each creature develops memories throughout its life.

The memory system should include:

Owner interactions.

Important conversations.

Feeding and care history.

Battles and victories.

Battles lost.

Missions completed.

Periods of neglect.

Important milestones.

Awakening history.

The creature should be able to refer to past experiences naturally.

For example, it might remember losing an important battle, being fed after a long absence or completing a difficult expedition.

3.3 Autonomous behaviour

The creature should have a daily routine.

It can independently:

Sleep.

Eat.

Explore its room.

Play.

Become bored.

React to its surroundings.

Make comments.

Create journal entries.

Display different moods.

Higher $SOUL holder tiers can unlock more advanced autonomous behaviour and deeper AI capabilities.

The AI should not need to generate every movement or animation. Most behaviour should be handled by a deterministic game system.

AI generation should be reserved for meaningful interactions, conversations and events.

This keeps operating costs under control.

4. Care and Creature Needs

The Tamagotchi aspect gives players a reason to return.

Each creature has several needs:

Hunger.

