7. Main Gameplay: Arena

The Arena is the central competitive activity.

There is one battle system, shared by cats and awakened girls.

Awakening does not increase stats or change battle calculations.

7.1 Creature battles

The initial system focuses on 1v1 battles.

The engine evaluates:

Creature attributes.

Personality.

Energy.

Happiness.

Hunger.

Mood.

Other battle conditions.

For example, a tired or hungry creature may perform worse.

Rarity should only have a small influence, preventing the arena from becoming a contest of who hatched the rarest creature.

The battle engine determines the result.

AI can provide commentary, tactical dialogue and reactions, but it does not decide the winner.

7.2 Free battles

Free battles should be available to everyone.

They provide:

Experience.

Ranking progression.

Achievements.

Gameplay rewards.

Battle history.

This gives non-paying users a meaningful reason to participate.

A free battle uses the same engine as a paid one. The odds do not improve because money is on the table.

7.3 Paid battles

Paid battles are the same 1v1, with a pot.

Both players lock SOL into the match. The winner receives the pot. CRITTERS takes a percentage off the top. That percentage is the fee. It is shown before either player signs, and it is the same percentage on every table.

The flow:

The challenger locks their buy-in.

The other owner has until the next day to match it.

If they do not match, the buy-in unlocks. Nobody is forced to fight.

When both sides are in, the engine settles the scrap.

The winner is paid from the pot. The fee is taken in that same settlement.

A sleeping, starving, or exhausted creature cannot be entered. The machine does not take the SOL. Personality can still refuse.

Cats and awakened girls sit the same tables. Waking does not change the fee, the odds, or the size of the pot.

Free battles stay open for practice, rank, and history. Paid battles are how a player wins SOL. The public record shows SOL won and lost beside the win count.

The engine publishes that percentage as 5% (`POT_FEE`). It is one number. It does not change per player, per rarity, or because she is awake. Changing the fee means changing that constant.

Any table that pays a monetary prize needs legal review in the jurisdictions where it is offered.

7.4 Rankings and competitions

The Arena includes:

