10. $SOUL Holder Benefits

10.1 Existing token, not an in-game currency

$SOUL is the existing Pump.fun token.

CRITTERS will not create a new token economy.

There will be:

No new in-game token.

No $SOUL staking.

No token locking.

No fixed token yield.

No token rewards for playing.

No requirement to spend $SOUL to play.

No token requirement for awakening.

No holder reward paid in $SOUL, SOL, or any other token.

The token launched on pump.fun. CRITTERS does not control the supply, so it cannot emit, airdrop, or pay holders in tokens. A perk that required new tokens cannot exist.

Instead, CRITTERS reads the $SOUL balance of the connected wallet.

That balance determines in-game privileges: food, crystals, and how comfortable the day is to play. The privileges are never a payout.

The token is an external eligibility check, not an in-game payment currency, and not a reward.

10.2 Holder verification

The process:

Player connects a wallet.

Player hatches a creature.

The wallet is associated with the creature.

CRITTERS reads the wallet's $SOUL holdings.

The holder tier is calculated.

Eligible benefits are activated.

Balances are periodically checked again.

The system should verify balances server-side using the official token mint address.

The player interface should clearly show the current holder tier and which benefits are unlocked.

10.3 Holder benefits

Creature appearance is determined by DNA. Holder benefits must not modify the cat or awakened girl's appearance.

Benefits are things the creature consumes or feels during play. They are not tokens, and they are not paid out.

A zero balance still has the full creature. Holding makes the day easier. It does not make her hit harder, and it does not wake her.

What a holder notices:

Food. The first feed of the day fills her more. She still has to be fed. A larger holding fills that meal further. The gain gets smaller as the bag grows, and she can still be hungry again by evening.

Crystals. A completed care action or expedition pays a bonus in crystals. A bigger bag raises that daily bonus. No play means no bonus. Crystals stay inside the game. They cannot be withdrawn or swapped for $SOUL.

Energy. She refills faster, and she still cannot pass full. A bigger bag shortens the wait. It does not add a free paid entry, and it does not change the scrap.

The machine. The case mark gets richer as the bag grows: a stitch, then a lamp, then more light. It is not part of her coat, and it dims if the balance falls.

Talk. More lines in a day as the holding grows, still spoken from the personality she already has. This is usage of the AI, not a token.

What holding must not do:

Pay $SOUL, SOL, or any other token.

Raise battle attributes or change who wins a pot.

Change rarity, DNA, hair, or outfit.

Set the awakening flag, or skip a neglected day.

Turn a bigger bag into a copy of a smaller one. More tokens always move food, crystals, refill, talk, or the case mark. They never move the scrap.

A one-block buy does not count. The tier uses a rolling average of the wallet balance.

10.4 Holder tiers

A tier system can make larger holdings progressively more valuable.

Illustrative structure:

Tier

What changes in play

None

The standard day. She is whole.

Jar

The first meal fills a little more. A small crystal bonus on something she actually did. A thin mark on the case.

Stocked

The meal fills more. The crystal cap is higher. Energy refills a little faster.

Pantry

A full meal, a strong crystal bonus, a fast refill, and more talk. This is a named step, not a ceiling.

Above the pantry

The same dials keep moving. Each time the bag doubles, the meal fills a little more, the crystal bonus rises a little, the refill gets a little faster, she has a few more lines, and the case mark gets brighter. The next doubling adds less than the one before. A much larger bag is still visibly ahead of a smaller one.

These steps are how the curve is explained. They are not a payout ladder, and they are not a wall. The balance that opens Jar, Stocked, and Pantry has to be set from the real $SOUL supply and who already holds it. Above the pantry the game reads the average balance directly, so two large holders are not treated as equal.

The benefits are cumulative. A higher holding keeps everything a smaller one already has.

10.5 Balance verification

The system should avoid temporary wallet transfers solely to unlock benefits.

Possible implementation:

Periodic balance snapshots.

A minimum holding period.

A rolling average balance for tier eligibility.

Clear tier downgrade rules.

Server-side verification.

The exact holding-period rules need to be decided before implementation.

The creature's identity and NFT ownership should remain separate from holder eligibility. If ownership changes in a future system, holder benefits should be recalculated according to the new eligible wallet.

11. Business Model and Revenue

CRITTERS needs to generate enough revenue to cover hosting, AI inference, development, maintenance, marketing and future content.

The game should be free to start and enjoyable without paying.

Revenue should come from optional premium services and content.

Importantly, $SOUL holdings are not project revenue. A user buying tokens on a secondary market does not automatically generate revenue for CRITTERS. Holder perks are delivered inside the game. They are never a token emission, because the project does not hold a supply to emit.

11.1 Paid battle fee

The first revenue is the percentage taken from a paid battle pot.

Players fund the pot. The winner receives the rest. CRITTERS receives only the published percentage, taken when the match settles.

That fee is not a yield, and it is not paid in $SOUL. It is a cut of SOL the two players chose to lock.

The free table does not generate this fee. A creature that cannot legally be entered does not lock SOL.

11.2 Premium AI subscription

This is potentially one of the strongest recurring revenue sources.

The standard creature experience remains available for free.

A premium subscription can provide:

More AI conversations.

Extended memory.

More advanced personality responses.

Longer conversations.

Voice interaction.

Additional autonomous behaviour.

More detailed journal entries.

The subscription pays for additional AI infrastructure and usage.

Holder tiers can include selected AI privileges, but the subscription can provide additional usage beyond those benefits.

11.3 Premium expeditions

Optional premium expeditions can introduce:

Additional challenges.

Special content.

Longer missions.

Unique gameplay experiences.

Exclusive achievements.

They should not provide a direct route to awakening or guaranteed superior combat attributes.

11.4 Marketplace

The marketplace should focus on tradable game items and collectibles.

Potential items include:

