16. Core Design Rules

These rules should guide every future development decision.

A creature's identity is permanent.

One wallet, one hatch, one roll. The same wallet cannot hatch again for a better rarity. Hatch does not spend AI.

A generated wallet is not a hatch. The hatch is stored only after that wallet's signed transaction confirms. The bot pays the network. Mass minting does not create model calls, image files, or a sellable legendary.

DNA determines its appearance.

AI does not change DNA or personality.

The cat and awakened girl are the same soul.

Awakening cannot be purchased.

Awakening does not improve combat stats.

Rarity does not determine whether awakening is possible.

$SOUL is not an in-game currency.

$SOUL holdings unlock in-game privileges that keep growing with the bag: a fuller meal, more crystals from play, a faster refill, a richer case mark, and more lines of talk. The growth slows as the bag gets larger. It does not stop.

Holding benefits are never paid in $SOUL, SOL, or any other token. The project does not control supply.

Holding benefits cannot modify creature appearance.

Holding benefits should not create direct combat-stat advantages, and they do not change who takes a pot.

Paid battles pay the winner the pot minus one published percentage. That percentage is the fee.

Free gameplay must remain meaningful.

AI must never determine battle outcomes.

AI must never directly execute financial transactions.

Creature-to-creature relationships are not part of the game.

Gameplay rules must be deterministic and server-validated.

Revenue must come from sustainable products and services, not artificial token rewards.

All gameplay should support Companion, Arena or Exploration.

New features should not introduce separate games without a compelling reason.

